  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 1
    m_Localized: After the synthesis and processing of glucagon, this hormone is
      transported to which part of the body to perform its function?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2
    m_Localized: Blood flow
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3
    m_Localized: Pancreas
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4
    m_Localized: Liver
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5
    m_Localized: Skin cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6
    m_Localized: The function of glucagon is to assist in the breakdown of glycogen
      to increase the body's glucose levels. In which organ does glucagon act to
      achieve this?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7
    m_Localized: Pancreas
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 8
    m_Localized: Liver
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 9
    m_Localized: Skin granules
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 10
    m_Localized: Blood flow
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11
    m_Localized: What happens when the glucose level decreases in the body?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 12
    m_Localized: Glucagon exits the granules of the skin and heads to the pancreas
      to release glucose
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13
    m_Localized: Glucagon is released from the lysosomes and heads to the liver to
      release glucose
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 14
    m_Localized: Glucagon heads to the bloodstream and carries glucose molecules
      into the cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15
    m_Localized: Glucagon is released from the granules of the pancreas and, in the
      liver, breaks down glycogen releasing glucose.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16
    m_Localized: After the synthesis and processing of acid hydrolase, to which cellular
      structure is this protein destined to perform its function?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17
    m_Localized: Lysosomes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 18
    m_Localized: Golgi apparatus
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19
    m_Localized: Endoplasmic reticulum
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 20
    m_Localized: Cytoplasm
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 21
    m_Localized: Acid hydrolases are proteins that act in the degradation of microorganisms
      that penetrate our body. After being synthesized, to which cells are they transported?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 22
    m_Localized: Bloodstream cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 23
    m_Localized: Pancreatic cells, where they will carry out the degradation of microorganisms
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 24
    m_Localized: Lysosomes, within the same cell where they were synthesized.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 25
    m_Localized: Skin cells, where they will phagocytize microorganisms.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 26
    m_Localized: 'After the synthesis and processing of proinsulin, the hormone insulin
      is secreted to:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 27
    m_Localized: Skin cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 28
    m_Localized: Blood flow
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 29
    m_Localized: Liver
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30
    m_Localized: Lysosomes
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 31
    m_Localized: Insulin's function is to decrease the levels of circulating glucose
      that increase after meals. How is it transported to the different tissues of
      the body?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 32
    m_Localized: Through the respiratory system
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 33
    m_Localized: Through the urinary system
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 34
    m_Localized: Through the endocrine system
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 35
    m_Localized: Through the circulatory system
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 36
    m_Localized: What happens when the glucose level increases in the body?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 37
    m_Localized: Insulin is released from the granules of the pancreas into the bloodstream
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 38
    m_Localized: Glucagon is released from the granules of the pancreas and heads
      to the bloodstream
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 39
    m_Localized: Insulin is released from the lysosomes to act on the cells of the
      pancreas
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 40
    m_Localized: Melanin is released from the granules and acts on the skin cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 41
    m_Localized: After the synthesis and processing of melanin, in which part of
      the body will this protein perform its function?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 42
    m_Localized: Liver
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 43
    m_Localized: Stomach
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 44
    m_Localized: Blood flow
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 45
    m_Localized: Cells and structures of the skin
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 46
    m_Localized: 'Melanin is a pigment produced by the action of the enzyme tyrosinase
      on the amino acid tyrosine, which protects the body from solar radiation. To
      perform its function, this pigment needs to be transported to specific tissues
      of the body such as:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 47
    m_Localized: Only the skin cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 48
    m_Localized: Skin cells (epithelium) and hair follicles
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 49
    m_Localized: Blood cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 50
    m_Localized: Nail cells
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 51
    m_Localized: In which structure of the skin cells is melanin accumulated?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 52
    m_Localized: In the lysosomes.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 53
    m_Localized: In the mitochondria.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 54
    m_Localized: In the melanocytes.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55
    m_Localized: In the ribosomes.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 56
    m_Localized: Glucagon is another hormone produced by the pancreas in the form
      of proglucagon in cells known as the Islets of Langerhans. When blood sugar
      levels are low (hypoglycemia), proglucagon is converted into glucagon, which
      is released into the bloodstream and will act on liver cells. Glucagon will
      act on the liver by breaking down glycogen molecules (glucose polymer), stored
      in this organ, to release glucose into circulation.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 57
    m_Localized: Acid hydrolases are enzymes produced by phagocytes, which after
      their synthesis and processing, are directed to the Golgi apparatus and stored
      in vesicles in the cytoplasm. When cells undergo the process of phagocytosis
      (engulfment of foreign molecules in the body, bacteria, viruses), the material
      is stored in vesicles called phagosomes. The vesicles containing hydrolases
      (lysosomes) + phagosomes fuse so that the foreign particles can be degraded.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 58
    m_Localized: Insulin is synthesized in the pancreas in the form of a precursor
      called proinsulin and is stored in the cells of this organ. To become active,
      it undergoes a breakdown ("processing") and is stored in secretory granules
      of the pancreas. When blood glucose levels increase, proinsulin is converted,
      with the help of enzymes, into active insulin, which is then released into
      the bloodstream to perform its physiological function, namely, participating
      in the process of transporting glucose into cells in different tissues of the
      body.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 59
    m_Localized: Melanin is a pigment produced by cells called melanocytes, which
      gives color to the skin, hair, and eyes. The amino acid tyrosine undergoes
      a process of oxidation in these cells by an enzyme called tyrosinase, which
      transforms it into melanin, resulting in skin pigmentation. The amount of melanin
      we produce is genetically determined, which is why people have skin, hair,
      and eyes of varying color intensities. It protects the DNA of our cells from
      the effects of solar radiation, which is why we tan when exposed to sunlight,
      meaning that the highest amount of melanin is produced on these occasions.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
